<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Vannius</h1><p class="st-meta">19 CE – 50 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Roman client kingdom of the Quadi north of the Danube, set up to house the followers of the fallen kings Maroboduus and Catualda.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maroboduus">Maroboduus</a> and his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catualda">Catualda</a> were driven out in 19, their followers were settled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> between the March (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morava-river">Morava</a>) and Váh rivers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drusus-the-younger">Drusus the Younger</a> installed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vannius">Vannius</a>, of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/quadi">Quadi</a>, as their king, creating a buffer state opposite the Roman frontier in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-noricum">Noricum</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vannius">Vannius</a> ruled for some thirty years as a client of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> says he was at first respected, then grew arrogant, enriching himself through raiding and tolls on traders. The kingdom grew wealthy on commerce along the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jaipur-state">Amber</a> Road between the Baltic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carnuntum">Carnuntum</a>, and Roman goods reached its elite graves.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 50 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vannius">Vannius</a> was overthrown by his nephews <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vangio">Vangio</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sido">Sido</a> with the help of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hermunduri">Hermunduri</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lugii">Lugii</a>. Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> refused to intervene but gave him refuge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a>. The nephews divided the kingdom and stayed loyal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>; Sido fought for Vespasian in 69.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-vannius.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Vannius" width="320" height="216"><figcaption>Image: Zello · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Quadi_settlement_area.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>19 CE – 50 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Vannius, Vangio, Sido</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Germanic paganism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Elbe Germanic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/slovakia">Slovakia</a>, <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>, <a href="/countries/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Regnum Vannianum</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=34&amp;focus=kingdom-of-vannius" aria-label="Kingdom of Vannius on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5117 479 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Vannius lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5437 735h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5437 735h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Slovakia. <a href="/atlas?year=34&amp;focus=kingdom-of-vannius">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
